1. (a). Exact condition that causes dead lock
The deadlock occurs because P1 and P2 acquire the resources in different orders.
Execution sequence:
P1 locks R1.
P2 locks R2.
Both processes wait for 100 ms.
P1 tries to lock R2, but it is already held by P2.
P2 tries to lock R1, but it is already held by P1.
Each process waits indefinitely for the other to release the resource.
This creates a circular wait, resulting in a deadlock.
The four Coffman conditions for deadlock are present:
Mutual Exclusion 
Hold and Wait 
No Preemption 
Circular Wait 
(b) Optimization to eliminate deadlock
Use a fixed resource ordering (Lock Ordering).
Both processes should acquire resources in the same order.
Example:
Process P1:
lock(R1)
lock(R2)
Critical Section
unlock(R2)
unlock(R1)
Process P2:
lock(R1)
lock(R2)
Critical Section
unlock(R2)
unlock(R1)
Since both processes request resources in the same order, circular wait cannot occur, eliminating deadlock without changing the program logic.
Q2. CPU Utilization Problem (10 Marks)
(a) Cause of low CPU utilization
Although processes are ready, they repeatedly move between:
Ready → Waiting → Ready → Waiting
This indicates that the processes are I/O-bound.
Reasons:
Processes frequently request I/O operations.
CPU remains idle while waiting for I/O completion.
CPU utilization drops to 30% because most time is spent waiting instead of executing.
(b) System-level optimization
Increase multiprogramming level
Keep more processes in memory.
While one process waits for I/O, another process uses the CPU.
Use asynchronous I/O or overlap I/O with computation
Allows CPU execution while I/O operations are in progress.
Reduces idle CPU time.
Result:
Better CPU utilization
Higher throughput
Improved system performance
Q3. Memory Bottleneck Debugging (10 Marks)
(a) Memory issue causing performance degradation
Memory usage:
Code = 5 MB
Heap = 150 MB
Stack = 1 MB × 25 threads = 25 MB
Total memory:
5 + 150 + 25 = 180 MB
Although 180 MB is much smaller than 2 GB RAM, the program experiences frequent page faults because:
The large heap causes poor memory locality.
Multiple threads access different memory pages.
Working set exceeds cache efficiency, increasing page faults and reducing performance.
(b) Two optimizations
1. Reduce heap usage

Free unused memory.
Use efficient data structures.
Reuse memory buffers.
2. Reduce thread stack size or thread count

Lower stack size if possible.
Use a thread pool instead of many threads.
These optimizations reduce memory consumption and page faults.
Q4. Starvation Debugging (10 Marks)
(a) Root cause of starvation
The scheduler uses a Multilevel Queue Scheduling algorithm:
Q1 → Interactive (Highest Priority)
Q2 → System
Q3 → Batch (Lowest Priority)
Process P5 is in Q3.
Whenever new processes arrive in Q1 or Q2, they are always executed first.
As a result:
P5 continuously waits.
It never gets CPU time.
This is called starvation (indefinite blocking).
(b) Optimization to ensure fairness
Use Aging.
Gradually increase the priority of processes waiting for a long time.
Eventually move P5 to a higher-priority queue.
Example:
P5 waits for long time
↓
Priority increases
↓
Moves from Q3 → Q2 → Q1
↓
Gets CPU
Benefits:
Prevents starvation.
Ensures fairness.
Improves response time for long-waiting processes.
Q5. Context-Switch Overhead Debugging (10 Marks)
(a) Why is context switching so high?
Given:

20,000 context switches/sec
Average CPU burst = 2 ms
Time quantum = 1 ms
Very high I/O frequency
Reasons:
1.Very small time quantum (1 ms)
A thread is preempted before completing its 2 ms CPU burst.
Every thread requires multiple context switches.
2.Frequent I/O operations
Threads frequently block for I/O.
Each block and wake-up causes additional context switches.
As a result, the CPU spends significant time saving and restoring process states instead of executing useful work.
(b) Two optimizations
1. Increase the time quantum
Example:
1 ms → 4 ms or 5 ms
Benefits:
Fewer preemptions.
Reduced context-switch overhead.
Better CPU efficiency.
2. Reduce I/O blocking
Use asynchronous I/O.
Batch I/O operations.
Buffer data to reduce the number of I/O requests.
Benefits:
Fewer waiting states.
Fewer context switches.
Higher throughput.
